ESP32 iPhone Agent Hybrid
=========================

This repository combines two MIT projects and one GPL-3.0 firmware subtree.
The computer-side code does not link the GPL code. It talks to the board
over a serial protocol and a TCP video port.

Computer-side code (MIT)
------------------------
`server/`, `scripts/`, `skills/`, `prompts/`, `docs/`, and the root README are
MIT, under the copyrights in `LICENSE`:

- Copyright (c) 2026 Kz-Aven
- Copyright (c) 2026 Henry Yuan

The Python server started from Kz-Aven/esp32-to-iphone
(https://github.com/Kz-Aven/esp32-to-iphone, commit 5e7b4c2), which itself
descends from the legacy server of ESP32 iPhone Agent. Tool names, the
Chinese Pinyin compose flow, calibration, and single-use `screen_id`
observations come from that project. The screen transport and the device
transport were replaced: TCP port 7201 decoded with PyAV, and the CH343
serial port instead of UxPlay and the old TCP port 7300.

Firmware (GPL-3.0 as a whole)
-----------------------------
`hardware/hid_bridge/` is the HID bridge from:

  ESP32 iPhone Agent by Henry Yuan
  https://github.com/onehumanbeing/esp32-iphone-agent
  commit 5a4fddf

The Handsy project as a whole is MIT (see upstream `LICENSE` and `NOTICE`).
`hardware/hid_bridge/src/airplay/` is a GPL-3.0 port of the UxPlay AirPlay
receiver (itself derived from RPiPlay and playfair). See
`hardware/hid_bridge/src/airplay/LICENSE-GPL.md` and
`hardware/hid_bridge/src/airplay/playfair/LICENSE.md`.

Because the firmware binary links that directory, the firmware source tree
in this repository and any firmware binary built from it are distributed
under GPL-3.0. If you give someone a flashed board or a firmware file, you
must also offer them the corresponding complete firmware source.

Do not copy `src/airplay/` or `playfair` into the MIT Python tree.

